{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>מחרף עצל לא יחרוש ושאל בקציר ואין (משלי כ, ד)</b>" "<b>King Solomon,</b> peace be upon him, expounded in the book of Mishlei to clarify the general principles of all good traits, so that a person would distance himself from dispraiseworthy traits and draw near to the desirable ones. Among them are the traits of diligence and laziness, for the trait of laziness is a root of many harms and a cause of numerous stumbling-blocks. There is a sluggard in his house, and a sluggard in his body, and a sluggard in his soul, and a sluggard in Torah and in commandments and in good deeds. There is a sluggard in his house: one who needs to repair a ceiling in his house — if he repairs it at once he can repair it with a small outlay, but if he is lazy in the matter and neglects it, in the end he will need to spend all his money until he is impoverished and sold off; and of this Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 10:18): ״בעצלתים ימך המקרה״. There is a sluggard in his body: if a person exerts himself in the affairs of his body and his food needs, and rejoices in his portion, and is satisfied with the middling measure and does not seek luxuries, then he can attain them; but if he is lazy in them and does not think of them at all and relies on a miracle, he will die of hunger; and of this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Mishlei 13:4): ״מתאוה ואין נפשו עצל״. There is a sluggard in his soul, that is, he is lazy in the correction of his traits, for the soul in its traits is like the analogy of land: just as there is good land that is easy to work, and there is land whose working is difficult — yet even so the worker need not abandon it to ruin and relax in it, but rather labor in it according to his strength; and of this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (ibid. 18): ״גם מתרפה במלאכתו אח הוא לבעל משחית״. So too with souls: there is a good soul that accepts discipline with a few rebukes and a little effort, and there is a soul difficult to guide toward following the intellect; and if he exerts himself and labors in correcting his soul's traits like the labor of the worker in his land, there is no doubt that he will draw it little by little toward good traits; and of this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (ibid. 24): ״על שדה איש עצל עברתי ועל כרם אדם חסר לב״ — he compared desire to a field because it is born of matter, and if he does not guide it properly and leaves desire to its own course, he destroys his body and his soul; this is (ibid.): ״וגדר אבניו נהרסה״. There is a sluggard in Torah and in commandments and in good deeds: if he is lazy in the wisdom of Torah and does not exert himself in commandments and good deeds in this world, with what will his soul merit the World to Come? And this is what is said here: מחרף עצל לא יחרוש ושאל בקציר ואין — he says that the sluggard does not exert himself in what he needs at that time which is fit for exertion; and he compared every sluggard in whatever matter it may be to a worker of land who sits idle in winter, which is the time of plowing, and does not plow. And he compared this world to winter, which is the time of the dominion of the moon, and the World to Come, which is the essence — he compared it to summer, which is the dominion of the sun; and it is well known to every discerning person that the sun and the moon are above as a model (דוגמא), and the sun is the essence. And so we find in Shir ha-Shirim that Solomon, peace be upon him, compared the exile to winter, as he said (Shir ha-Shirim 2:11): ״כי הנה הסתו עבר״ וגו'. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, already expounded: this world resembles the eve of Shabbat, and the World to Come resembles Shabbat — one who exerted on the eve of Shabbat will eat on Shabbat; one who did not exert, from where will he eat? They explained to us that just as the day of Shabbat is a day of rest, not a day of action and toil, so too the World to Come is a world of rest and recompense, not a world of action; and from this they said, if he did not exert, from where will he eat? And this is ושאל בקציר ואין. And what he said, מחורף and not בחורף — just as he said בקציר — its meaning is 'because of the fear of winter,' for so is the way of the sluggard that he makes an excuse for his refrainment from the matter, in the manner of what is written (Mishlei 26:13): ״אמר עצל שחל בדרך״; and what he avoids in plowing because of his laziness, he attributes to the fear of the cold. And Solomon's intention in his constant disparagement of the trait of laziness is to make it repulsive in the eyes of people, that they should distance themselves from it and draw near to the trait of diligence; and this is what he proves and warns through the ant (ibid. 6): ״לך אל נמלה עצל ראה דרכיה וחכם״ — he says, even though the ant is the lowliest of creeping things, from it a person can learn a matter of wisdom regarding the diligence that is in it, that it gathers at one time what it will eat at another time when it cannot gather; and furthermore, the grain upon which rain has fallen — she brings it out into the wind so that it dries, and afterward she gathers it and it is fit to be preserved; and he said (ibid.): ״אשר אין לה קצין שוטר ומושל״ — the intention in this is that it has no intellect at all, for all that it does is not by force of intellect but by force of nature alone. And because the intellect has three names — they are חכמה (wisdom), תבונה (understanding), and דעת (knowledge) — and they are attributed only to the human species alone, and all three are one kind, therefore he repeated three names of dominion (קצין שוטר ומושל), all of which are one kind. And so, with the trait of diligence a person can acquire the life of this world, and with laziness he can lose it; and with diligence he can acquire the life of the World to Come, and with laziness he will lose it. With the trait of diligence he fulfills the commandments and there will come to him from them life and good and blessing, and with laziness he brings upon himself death and evil and curse — for the prerogative is given to him and everything depends on his choice and his will, and all matters are entrusted to his hand to do good or evil; and then blessing will come to him for the good and curse for the evil. And this is what is written:" "<b>ראה אנכי נותן לפניכם היום ברכה וקללה.</b> He said ראה ('see') in the singular and לפניכם ('before you') in the plural, for Moses addresses everyone — both the individuals and the multitude. And since the blessing and the curse emanate from two attributes by which the world was created, and they are the attribute of judgment and the attribute of mercy, whose nature is revealed only to discerning individuals, therefore he said ראה in the singular — and it is seeing with the eye of the intellect, in the manner of (Ecclesiastes 1:16): ״ולבי ראה הרבה חכמה ודעת״. And since the blessing and the curse are an incentive for the multitude in the fulfillment of commandments and to instill fear in them when they transgress them, therefore he addressed the multitude and said לפניכם. And so he said in another place (Deuteronomy 4:5): ״ראה למדתי אתכם חקים ומשפטים״ — he mentioned ראה in the singular corresponding to the חקים (statutes), whose rationale is known only to individuals, and he mentioned אתכם in the plural, for the Torah in its statutes and judgments was given to the many. Thus it has been explained in this verse that the prerogative and the choice are in the hand of man — whether he wishes to guide himself on a straight path or to stand on a path that is not good, everything is entrusted to his will and everything depends on his choice. And from this Scripture says (ibid. 5): ״מי יתן והיה לבבם זה להם ליראה אותי״ — this teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not decree upon a person to be righteous or wicked, to do good or evil, but rather everything is in his hand. For if the Holy One, blessed be He, decreed upon a person to be righteous or wicked, or if some factor drew him by the very nature of his origin toward one of the ways, good or evil, how could the Holy One, blessed be He, command us through His prophets to do thus, since from the beginning of his creation it was already decreed upon him, or his nature draws him to a matter from which it is impossible to deviate? And what place would there be for the entire Torah — to punish the wicked or to reward the righteous with good reward? And this is a great and mighty principle, and it is the foundation of the faith. And because of this, this great principle is mentioned at the opening of the Torah and at its conclusion. That is, you find regarding the first man written (Genesis 3:22): ״הן האדם היה כאחד ממנו לדעת טוב ורע״ — its meaning: the human species was unique in the world, and it has no second species that resembles it, neither among the supernal beings nor among the lower ones; for the supernal beings are intellectual, serving and praising HaShem, may He be exalted, with intellect and not by nature, and the lower beings also praise Him, blessed be He, by nature and not with intellect at all; and each and every one is compelled in its praise — this one is compelled by intellect and that one is compelled by nature; neither of them has any hindrance. But man is a composite of them and constituted from both: from the intellectual beings by virtue of the soul, and from the lower beings by virtue of nature; and he is not compelled but rather voluntary — for the prerogative is given in his hand to bring upon himself punishment and reward, blessing and curse, at its conclusion. That is, you find at the end of the words of Moses (Deuteronomy 30:15): ״ראה נתתי לפניך היום את החיים ואת הטוב ואת המות ואת הרע״ — that is, that the prerogative is in your hand to do good or evil; and so he says here: ראה אנכי נותן לפניכם היום." "<b>And the Rambam,</b> of blessed memory, wrote: Do not wonder how it can say (Psalms 135:6): ״כל אשר חפץ ה' עשה בשמים ובארץ״ — certainly everything is done according to His will, even though our deeds are entrusted to us. How so? Just as it is the will of HaShem, may He be exalted, that fire and wind rise upward and water and earth descend below, and the sphere revolves in a circle, and likewise the rest of the world's creatures follow their course as He desired, so too HaShem desired that man's prerogative be in his hand and all his deeds entrusted to him, with no coercion or compulsion — rather, he himself, with the discernment that HaShem, may He be exalted, gave him, does all that he can. Therefore a person is judged according to his deeds: if he did good, it is done well to him, and if he did evil, it is done ill to him. As the prophet said (Isaiah 50:11): ״מידי היתה זאת לכם״; (ibid. 66): ״גם המה בחרו בדרכיהם״ וגו'. And of this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 11:9): ״שמח בחור בילדותך״ וגו' ״ודע כי על כל אלה יביאך האלהים במשפט״ — end of his quotation, of blessed memory."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:21.613661477Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}